  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 311 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery currently shows some shortwave energy \napproaching the Northern Rockies with a more robust upper low off of \nthe Pacific Northwest Coast. At the surface, high pressure is \ncentered over the Mid-Atlantic States with lee troughing continuing \nto deepen from eastern WY down through eastern CO.\n\nStill looking for gusty southwest winds across much of the area \ntoday, with speeds similar to what central KS had yesterday, which \nwas sustained 20-30 mph with gusts around 40 mph. Strongest winds \nlook to be late this morning through the early afternoon hours. \nWhile a few sites will likely flirt with wind advisory criteria, \nconfidence is not high enough to run with one at this time, and \nis mainly due to lack of deep mixing and limited period for the\nstrongest winds. By Thu morning, upper impulse will be lifting \nacross the Western Great Lakes region with or next upper trough \nabout to come onshore over CA. Even though we will remain in \nsoutherly return flow on Thu, winds won't be nearly as strong. \nHowever, this will change on Fri as lee troughing strengthens \nahead of Western CONUS upper trough. Wind speeds will likely be \nslightly stronger than they will be today, especially for \ncentral KS. Current thinking is sustained around 30 mph with \ngusts 40-45 mph. \n\nThere is decent model agreement that the main vort max with this \nsystem will track across northern NM/southern CO on Sat and across \nnorthern OK/southern KS Sat night. Still looking like the best rain \nchances will be over mainly eastern KS late Sat afternoon and \nespecially Sat evening. Confidence drops off considerably beyond Mon \nas models start to differ on the track of a southern stream system. \n\nConfidence remains high that above normal temps will be in place for \nToday through Sat with a few locations across southern and \nsoutheast KS possibly reaching 70 for Thu-Sat. Colder air will \nspill south behind the departing wave on Sun, knocking highs \ndown closer to seasonal normals for this time of year.",
